ased on their location. This may be the site of origin, but it can also represent a secondary site for metastatized cancer. We use the generic ''disease has location'' relation, which generalized over primary and secondary sites.\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/carcinoma.yaml:13:  We use the generic ''disease has location'' relation, which generalized over primary\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/specific_disease_by_disrupted_process.yaml:5:description: 'This is auto-generated. Add your description here\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/allergic_form_of_disease.yaml:7:  An etiological pattern that extends an etiology-generic disease to an allergic form\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/isolated.yaml:10:  the isolated/syndromic-neutral version. In general, this pattern should be used\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/malignant.yaml:6:  generic neoplasm class.\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/nonhuman_disease_series_by_gene.yaml:1:pattern_name: nonhuman_disease_series_by_gene\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/nonhuman_disease_series_by_gene.yaml:3:pattern_iri: http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/mondo/patterns/nonhuman_disease_series_by_gene.yaml\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/nonhuman_disease_series_by_gene.yaml:5:description: 'This pattern is for non-human diseases that are defined based on a variation in one gene, such as many genetic diseases from the Online Mendelian Inheritance in Animals (OMIA).  Examples: TBD '\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/nonhuman_disease_series_by_gene.yaml:13:  gene: SO:0000704\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/nonhuman_disease_series_by_gene.yaml:26:  gene: '''gene'''\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/nonhuman_disease_series_by_gene.yaml:33:  - gene\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/nonhuman_disease_series_by_gene.yaml:40:  - gene\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/nonhuman_disease_series_by_gene.yaml:46:  - gene\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/nonhuman_disease_series_by_gene.yaml:53:  - gene\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/nonhuman_disease_series_by_gene.yaml:57:  text: Any %s, occuring in %s, in which